WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.700
Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;Hello, my name is&nbsp;Margot
Lee Shetterly&nbsp;and I am the author of&nbsp;HIDDEN

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.030
FIGURES. Film Courage: How long did it take
to write and then sell the book? Margot Lee

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.630
Shetterly:&nbsp;All told it took about six years.
A little less than six years, actually. I

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:18.330
started working on it in December of 2010.
I really did about three years of research

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:24.820
before I put together a book proposal, finding
an agent and then finding a publisher. So

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:32.960
I submitted the final draft of this book in
July of this year. And the book was published

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.820
in September, a couple months before the movie
came out. Film Courage: So then how did the

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.460
movie come about? How was it discovered and
who wanted to take it on? I know Theodore

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.160
Melfi [and Allison Schroeder] did the screenplay
(the adaptation). Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;My

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:56.590
literary agent is&nbsp;Mackenzie Brady Watson&nbsp;and
some how it landed on the desk of&nbsp;Donna Gigliotti&nbsp;who

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:03.079
is the producer of the movie. So Donna called
me up and remember this was only a book proposal

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.830
this time. It was like three years ago and
I hadn’t finished the book. She saw the

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:13.320
book proposal and she said&nbsp;“We are going
to make a movie of this story.”&nbsp;And I thought

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.040
“Well, geez. I’m still writing the book.
It’s not even a book. You want to option

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.210
my book proposal? I’d never written a book
before.” But she believe in that story.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:34.110
She really took a flyer on a first time writer/author
and now the rest is history. Everything that

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.320
she said on this first phone call has come
true. Film Courage: I know HIDDEN FIGURES

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.560
is a true story that many did not know of
before it came out. What movie did you see

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.020
which had the same type of impact that I’m
sure many people will feel once they see the

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.850
film [HIDDEN FIGURES]? Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;Wow!
That’s a hard question. There are so many

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:01.520
movies that are amazing and inspirational.
You kind of put me on the spot here. Film

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.879
Courage: [Laughs] Sorry! Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;You
know, I’d have to think about it. In general

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:14.659
the thing about HIDDEN FIGURES, even though
it is a work of non-fiction, it is totally

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:19.349
a work of imagination, you know? And I think
the thing about movies that you do remember

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:24.599
and that do inspire you is that they do spark
your imagination and I think that is the reason

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:28.830
people are responding so strongly is that
they see something in these women that says

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.209
to them “Wow! I just expanded my imagination
of what the world looks like and who I might

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:36.769
be.” Film Courage: And you would hear them
[the women working at NASA] speaking to you,

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:40.249
in your own way? In your research that you
did on them….did you hear their voices?

00:02:40.249 --> 00:02:45.389
Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;I did! It’s so funny
that you asked that question. I found myself

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:51.269
talking to them, having conversations with
them and dreaming about them, and thinking

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:56.069
about them. I looked at photos of them to
see what they looked like at different parts

00:02:56.069 --> 00:03:01.409
of their life. I listened to their children
talk about the gestures. Like Dorothy Vaughan.

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:06.949
When she got upset would start putting her
finger on the table and everybody knew to

00:03:06.949 --> 00:03:14.140
back off, you know? And that she was really,
like a fearless kind of a woman. I knew all

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:21.019
of these different things about them until
I felt that they had really come back to life

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:27.760
and that they were my friends. And they really
were. They become my companions. Of course

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.999
in the case of Katherine Johnson, she is 98-years-old
and she is very much still with us. And I

00:03:32.999 --> 00:03:39.779
had an incredible privilege to spend time
interviewing her and getting to know her life

00:03:39.779 --> 00:03:42.459
firsthand. Film Courage: Excellent! Thank
you! Best of luck and you look beautiful!

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:42.659
Margot Lee Shetterly:&nbsp;Thank you!

